To filter the event list based on the selected exercise, you'll need to implement a filtering mechanism in your application. Here’s a step-by-step guide to achieve this using JavaScript (assuming you're using a frontend framework like React or Vue.js):

### Step 1: Define Your Data Structure

First, ensure your data structure for events includes an `exercise` field. For example:

```javascript
const events = [
  { id: 1, name: 'Event 1', exercise: 'Squats' },
  { id: 2, name: 'Event 2', exercise: 'Push-ups' },
  { id: 3, name: 'Event 3', exercise: 'Squats' },
  // ...
];
```

### Step 2: Create State for Selected Exercise

Create state to store the currently selected exercise.

#### In React:

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

function EventList() {
  const [events, setEvents] = useState([
    { id: 1, name: 'Event 1', exercise: 'Squats' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Event 2', exercise: 'Push-ups' },
    { id: 3, name: 'Event 3', exercise: 'Squats' },
  ]);

  const [selectedExercise, setSelectedExercise] = useState('');

  return (
    <div>
      <select value={selectedExercise} onChange={(e) => setSelectedExercise(e.target.value)}>
        <option value="">All Exercises</option>
        <option value="Squats">Squats</option>
        <option value="Push-ups">Push-ups</option>
        {/* Add more exercises as needed */}
      </select>

      <ul>
        {events
          .filter(event => selectedExercise ? event.exercise === selectedExercise : true)
          .map(event => (
            <li key={event.id}>{event.name}</li>
          ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}

export default EventList;
```

#### In Vue.js:

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <select v-model="selectedExercise">
      <option value="">All Exercises</option>
      <option value="Squats">Squats</option>
      <option value="Push-ups">Push-ups</option>
      <!-- Add more exercises as needed -->
    </select>

    <ul>
      <li v-for="event in filteredEvents" :key="event.id">{{ event.name }}</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  data() {
    return {
      events: [
        { id: 1, name: 'Event 1', exercise: 'Squats' },
        { id: 2, name: 'Event 2', exercise: 'Push-ups' },
        { id: 3, name: 'Event 3', exercise: 'Squats' },
      ],
      selectedExercise: '',
    };
  },
  computed: {
    filteredEvents() {
      if (!this.selectedExercise) {
        return this.events;
      }
      return this.events.filter(event => event.exercise === this.selectedExercise);
    },
  },
};
</script>
```

### Step 3: Apply Filtering Logic

In both examples above, the filtering logic is applied in the JSX/Vue template. The `filter` method is used to create a new array containing only the events that match the selected exercise. If no exercise is selected (`selectedExercise` is empty), all events are shown.

### Step 4: Update the UI Based on Filtered Events

The filtered events are then rendered in the UI. Each event is displayed within a `<li>` element.

This setup ensures that the event list dynamically updates based on the selected exercise, providing a seamless user experience.
